/** @page oracleora_native_bulk_loadguide Native OCI Direct Path Bulk Loading

    @tableofcontents

    When built against a %Qore library with the native bulk-load DBI API, this driver implements
    \c DBI_CAP_HAS_BULK_LOAD with OCI Direct Path Load.  The path is insert-only and explicitly
    opt-in through BulkSqlUtil; ordinary inserts and array binds remain the default.

    @code{.py}
%requires BulkSqlUtil
%requires SqlUtil

Datasource ds("oracle:user/password@db%host:1521");
SqlUtil::AbstractTable table = (new SqlUtil::Table(ds, "my_table")).getTable();
BulkSqlUtil::BulkInsertOperation op(table, {
    "bulk_load": True,       # require direct path; "auto" falls back when ineligible
    "block_size": 1000,
    "bulk_load_options": {
        "oracle_direct_path_buffer_size": 1048576,
    },
});
on_error {
    op.discard();
    ds.rollback();
}
map op.queueData($1), rows.iterator();
op.endStream();
    @endcode

    One direct-path context remains active across all buffered blocks.  Numeric, character, \c DATE,
    and non-time-zone \c TIMESTAMP columns are eligible when the table and column names are simple
    unquoted Oracle identifiers.  Quoted identifiers, \c RAW, LOB, \c LONG, interval, object,
    collection, \c REF, cursor, and time-zone timestamp columns are dynamically ineligible;
    \c "bulk_load": \c "auto" uses the ordinary insert path for these cases, while \c True reports
    that the required native path is unavailable.

    The following optional values can be passed in \c "bulk_load_options":
    - \c "oracle_direct_path_buffer_size": OCI stream buffer size in bytes; the minimum is 65536 and
      the default is 1048576
    - \c "oracle_direct_path_parallel": enables OCI parallel direct-path loading; defaults to \c False
    - \c "oracle_direct_path_nolog": requests OCI no-logging mode; defaults to \c False

    @warning OCI Direct Path Load has materially different transaction, trigger, constraint, and
    logging behavior from ordinary SQL.  In particular, successful \c OCIDirPathFinish() commits the
    direct-path load independently of \c Datasource::commit(), while abort leaves its rows invisible.
    Use this opt-in only where those Oracle semantics are acceptable.  Enabling parallel or no-logging
    mode can impose additional Oracle restrictions and operational requirements.

    The driver validates every hash-of-columns block, broadcasts scalar values, cooperatively checks
    cancellation during large conversions, and aborts the OCI context on errors, observer refusal,
    rollback, or connection close.  Native loads own mutation-observer stream reporting so events are
    emitted exactly once.  Consumed bytes are the encoded field payload supplied to OCI, excluding OCI
    framing; NULL fields contribute zero bytes.

    The \c Datasource::getServerVersion() and \c Datasource::getClientVersion()
    methods are implemented for this driver. \c Datasource::getServerVersion()
    returns a string giving server information similar to the following:

    @code{.py}
    Oracle Database 10g Release 10.2.0.1.0 - 64bit Production
    @endcode

    The \c Datasource::getClientVersion() returns a hash giving version
    information in the following keys: \c major, \c minor,
    \c update, \c patch, \c port_update.

    Note: There seems to be a bug in Oracle 9i and earlier in the streaming
    \c OCILobRead() function, where the LOB buffer must be at least twice
    as big as the LOB data to be read. This bug does not affect versions
    of the Qore oracle module linked with Oracle 10g libraries or later.

    @note Object (Oracle named type) and collection support is supplied
    courtesy of \c ocilib (http://orclib.sourceforge.net/), note that
    \c ocilib was highly modified to be usable in this module, mostly
    due to the fact that we use a separate environment data structure for
    each connection to ensure maximum thread scalability - the Oracle docs
    say that all operations on an environment handle or any handle derived
    from an environment handle (i.e. statement handles, etc) must be either
    wrapped in a mutex (when initialized with \c OCI_NO_MUTEX) or will
    be wrapped in a mutex by oracle (with \c OCI_THREADED and
    without \c OCI_NO_MUTEX).
*/
